---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import {SITE} from '../site';

const title = 'Privacy Policy: a Local-First Tab Manager | TabLodge';
const description =
	'TabLodge keeps your tab sessions and bookmarks on your device. No accounts, no TabLodge servers, no analytics. Optional sync goes only to a server you run. Read what it stores and why each permission is needed.';

const permissions = [
	['tabs', 'Reading tab URLs, titles and placement to save sessions; opening tabs to restore them.'],
	['tabGroups', 'Saving and recreating native tab groups.'],
	['bookmarks', 'Importing your browser bookmarks when you ask, and noticing a page you bookmark in the browser if you turn on that setting (off by default).'],
	['favicon (Chromium only)', 'Showing site icons from the browser\'s local cache.'],
	['storage, unlimitedStorage', 'Keeping your data in local extension storage without the default size cap.'],
	['contextMenus', 'Optional right-click save and bookmark actions.'],
	['clipboardWrite', 'Copying bookmark URLs and session links when you ask.'],
	['sidePanel (Chromium only)', 'Showing bookmarks, sessions and open tabs in the browser side panel. Firefox shows the same page in its sidebar, which needs no permission.'],
	['search', 'Running a web search with your default engine, only when you choose "Search the web".'],
	['alarms', 'Scheduling crash-recovery snapshots, automatic backups, trash bin cleanup, archiving old sessions, sync, and the idle lock of the private library.'],
	['idle', 'Noticing that you locked your screen, so the private library locks too. It reports only the lock state, never what you are doing.'],
	['activeTab, scripting', 'Used by the palette shortcut, which shows the palette over that one page when you press it (the default; Settings can switch to a window). No install warning, no site access, and nothing runs on pages until you press the shortcut.'],
	['downloads (optional)', 'Saving backup copies to your Downloads folder, only if you turn that on; turning it off gives the permission back.'],
];
---

<Base
	title={title}
	description={description}
	path="/privacy/"
	breadcrumbs={[
		{name: 'Home', path: '/'},
		{name: 'Privacy', path: '/privacy/'},
	]}
	jsonLd={[
		{
			'@type': 'WebPage',
			'@id': 'https://tablodge.bbay.dev/privacy/#webpage',
			url: 'https://tablodge.bbay.dev/privacy/',
			name: title,
			description,
			dateModified: '2026-11-12',
			isPartOf: {'@id': 'https://tablodge.bbay.dev/#website'},
		},
	]}
>
	<article class="narrow prose page">
		<nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> / <span aria-current="page">Privacy</span></nav>
		<h1>Privacy</h1>
		<p class="note">Last updated: 2026-11-12</p>
		<p class="lead">
			TabLodge keeps your sessions, bookmarks and settings in your browser profile. It has no accounts or backend, and <strong>does not collect your data.</strong> It uploads your library only to a sync server you run yourself, and only after you connect one.
		</p>

		<h2>What TabLodge does not do</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>No accounts and no TabLodge servers or cloud. Optional sync talks only to a server you run yourself; it is off until you enter its address and token, and that server stores your library in plain text. A separate private window library syncs only if you turn that on too, and is encrypted in the browser when it has a PIN or password.</li>
			<li>No analytics, telemetry, crash reporting or advertising.</li>
			<li>No network requests of its own unless you turn on sync. All code, styles, fonts and icons are bundled in the extension; nothing is loaded from a CDN.</li>
			<li>No website access. TabLodge requests no host permissions, declares no content scripts and never reads page contents. The search palette is drawn over a page only when you press its shortcut, and Settings can switch it to a window.</li>
			<li>No sale or sharing of data with anyone.</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>What TabLodge stores, and where</h2>
		<p>Everything is stored locally in your browser profile:</p>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>Extension storage</strong> (<code>chrome.storage.local</code>, keys <code>tablodge.state</code>, <code>tablodge.session.*</code> and <code>tablodge.bookmarks.*</code>): your saved sessions (tab titles, URLs, notes, pinned, tab-group and split-view info), folders, bookmark columns and bookmarks, settings, and excluded websites.</li>
			<li><strong>Extension storage</strong> (<code>uiTheme</code>, <code>uiAccent</code>) and the extension's <code>localStorage</code> (<code>tablodge.uiTheme</code>, <code>tablodge.uiAccent</code>): your Light, Dark or System appearance choice and accent color, and a copy used to paint each page in the right theme before it loads.</li>
			<li><strong>Recovery data</strong> (extension storage): rolling snapshots of your open windows for crash recovery, automatic and safety backups, the trash bin, and short-lived Undo copies (kept for two minutes). After an update that changes how your library is stored, a full copy of the old library is kept for seven days. Snapshots skip private windows and excluded websites.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			TabLodge reads tab details to save and restore sessions, find duplicates, show and search open tabs, and capture recovery snapshots. Snapshots are enabled by default and can be turned off in Settings, under Recovery &amp; backups. It reads your browser's bookmark tree only when you ask to import it. If you enable the browser bookmark setting, it also watches for newly created bookmarks to offer to add that page or add it automatically. That setting is off by default. Site icons come from the browser's local favicon cache. Firefox has no such cache, so there TabLodge shows an open tab's own icon where the browser provides one, and a letter tile otherwise.
		</p>
		<p>
			Saved sessions and bookmarks stay until you delete them. Trash is kept for 7, 30 or 90 days, depending on your setting. Snapshots, backups and Undo copies also have retention or size limits. You can clear recovery data in <strong>Settings &rsaquo; Recovery &amp; backups</strong>. Erasing sessions or bookmarks in <strong>Settings &rsaquo; Data</strong> creates a safety backup, so check the backup history too if you want to remove retained copies. Removing the extension deletes its browser storage.
		</p>
		<p>
			If you enable backup copies in Downloads, TabLodge writes JSON files to <code>Downloads/TabLodge/</code>. Turning the setting off or uninstalling does not delete those files. Exports, including JSON, HTML, CSV, Markdown, URL lists and full backup ZIP files, are not encrypted unless you protect a full backup with a PIN or password. If you do, TabLodge encrypts the file on your device (AES-256-GCM, with a key derived from your entry by PBKDF2) and stores neither the PIN or password nor a hint, so a lost one cannot be recovered. A PIN is weak because anyone with the file can try every PIN offline; use a password for sensitive data. Automatic backups and Downloads copies are never encrypted. Keep unprotected files private if their titles, URLs or notes are sensitive. A folder synced by another app may upload them.
		</p>
		<p>
			Opening a saved link loads that website normally. An optional setting, off by default, removes common tracking parameters from the destination when opening saved links or restoring sessions and snapshots. It leaves the URLs in your library unchanged. Choosing <strong>Search the web</strong> sends your query to the browser's default search engine. These are actions you request, not background reporting by TabLodge.
		</p>

		<h2>Private windows</h2>
		<p>The Firefox version does not run in private windows, so it cannot read or save them, and the rest of this section applies to Chromium browsers.</p>
		<p>After you enable Allow in Incognito in your browser's extension settings, the first private dashboard visit requires a storage choice. By default, private windows use the same library as regular windows, so private saves appear in the shared library. You can instead use a separate saved library in local extension storage or a separate temporary library in browser session storage. Temporary data clears on browser restart, not when private windows close. Both separate libraries start empty. Switching modes does not copy, merge or delete data. Appearance remains shared. New private tabs redirect to the TabLodge dashboard by default; you can turn this off in Settings under Private windows.</p>
		<p>The storage choice and redirect preference are kept in <code>tablodge.private.config</code>; separate library and recovery keys have the prefix <code>tablodge.private.</code>. The redirect preference does not record private browsing activity. Saved private data is not encrypted or hidden from someone with access to your browser profile unless you lock it. You can lock a separate saved library with a PIN or a password in Settings under Private windows. TabLodge then encrypts every value of it on your device, keeps the key only in memory until you lock it, the idle time passes, you lock your screen, the last private window closes or the browser closes, and stores no copy of the PIN or password, so a lost one cannot be recovered. A short PIN is weak against someone who copies your profile files, and exports from an unlocked private library are plain unless you protect a full backup. Temporary data also clears if the extension is disabled, reloaded or updated. Automatic snapshots, automatic backups and browser bookmark monitoring do not run in private windows. Explicit exports and manual backup downloads can still leave files on disk after temporary data clears. To erase a saved private library and its recovery copies, select that mode and open Settings from its private dashboard.</p>

		<h2>Permissions</h2>
		<div class="table-wrap">
			<table>
				<thead><tr><th scope="col">Permission</th><th scope="col">Used for</th></tr></thead>
				<tbody>
					{permissions.map(([name, use]) => <tr><th scope="row"><code>{name}</code></th><td>{use}</td></tr>)}
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>

		<h2>This website</h2>
		<p>
			This site (tablodge.bbay.dev) is a static page with no analytics, cookies or third-party scripts. It is hosted on GitHub Pages, which, like any web host, may keep ordinary server logs; see <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" rel="noopener">GitHub's privacy statement</a>. Manual release downloads are served by GitHub Releases. Store listings, installations, and updates are handled by Google through the <a href={SITE.chromeWebStore} rel="noopener">Chrome Web Store</a> and by Mozilla through <a href={SITE.firefoxAddons} rel="noopener">Firefox Add-ons</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>Contact</h2>
		<p>Questions or concerns: <a href={SITE.issues} rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>.</p>
	</article>
</Base>

<style>
	.page {
		padding-block: 3rem 5rem;
	}
	.crumbs {
		font-size: 0.9rem;
		color: var(--muted);
		margin-bottom: 1rem;
	}
</style>
